Herein, we report a surfactant-assisted solution synthesis of ultrathin 2D $c$ -MOF NSs $\cdot>500~\mathsf{m g}$ , $80\%-90\%$ yield) (Table S1†). The resultant 2D $c$ -MOF NSs are single-crystalline; the HHB$\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N i})$ NSs feature with a thickness of $4-5\mathsf{n m}$ (8-10 layers) and a lateral size of $0.25\mathrm{-}0.65~\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ , while the HHTP-Cu NSs possess a thickness of $\sim5.1{\pm}2.6\ \mathsf{n m}$ ( ${\sim}10$ layers) and a lateral size of $0.002\cdot0.02\ \upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ . As one typical example, few-layered HHB-Cu NSs present a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area (385 $\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ , much higher than the corresponding bulk particles (119 $\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ . To this end, HHB-Cu NSs are utilized as cathode materials for Li-ion batteries and present a specific capacity of $153\mathsf{\ m A}$ h ${\pmb g}^{-1}$ at potential windows of $1.3\substack{-2.6\mathrm{~V~}}$ (the specific capacity reached $400\mathsf{m A h}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at the potential window of 1.3-4.0 V) and exhibit high rate capability (charge within 3 min, $81\%$ , 1.0 A ${\pmb g}^{-1}$ vs. $0.1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\mathsf{]}$ and long-term cycling stability $90\%$ capacity retention after 1000 cycles), which are superior to its bulk powder sample (capacity, $40\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{h}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , rate capability, $25\%$ 1.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ vs. $0.1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , cycling stability, $65\%$ capacity retention after 50 cycles) and the thus-far reported MOF cathodes (Table $S2^{\dagger}$ ). Our work paves the way to develop conductive 2D $c$ -MOF NSs by scalable synthesis that accelerates the electrochemical performance as well as allows future facile solution-processing and integration into electronic devices.
## Results and discussion
The synthesis of ultrathin HHB-Cu NSs by a surfactant-assisted solution synthesis method is illustrated in Fig. 1A. For the first time, the anionic surfactant sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS) was employed to produce uniform ultrathin 2D $c$ -MOF nanosheets. In a typical synthesis, HHB-Cu NSs were obtained through the preparation of ${\mathsf{C u}}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}$ salts and SDS aqueous solution (1.7 mM) (Fig. 1B), followed by the addition of tetrahydroxy-1,4- quinone (THQ) ligands and then sonication the mixture at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 30 minutes (see ESI† for experimental details). The negatively charged hydrophilic tail of the SDS molecules will preferentially anchor onto the surface of MOFs through electrostatic interaction.15d Thus, the adsorbed SDS molecules can serve as structure directing agents to significantly alleviate the layer stacking, thus leading to the anisotropic growth of the MOFs in 2D direction and consequently the formation of ultrathin and large-sized nanosheets. In addition, the hydrophobic chains of SDS could not only weaken the interactions of interlayers and disassemble to form ultrathin nanosheets during the sonication treatment but also help to stabilize the as-synthesized HHB-Cu MOF NSs in solution to prevent further agglomeration.20-21 The solid product was collected and washed by water and ethanol in an ultrasonic ice bath for 30 min. Then, the upper colloidal suspension was collected and dried under vacuum for $12\mathsf{h}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to afford dark samples. The diluent colloid suspension of the obtained HHB-Cu NSs exhibited a Tyndall effect (inset in Fig. 1A); and due to their ultrathin nature, the colloid possessVeiedw Aertixcl elOlnelinet stability in the aqueous solution for at leaDsOt :610m.1o0n3t9/hDs.0SC01408G
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns in Fig. 1C exhibit strong reflections at $2\boldsymbol{\Theta}=7.8^{\circ}$ , $15.7^{\circ}$ , and $27.5^{\circ}$ , which can be indexed as the (100), (200) and (300) crystallographic planes of the HHB-Cu structure, respectively. This result reveals that the obtained product is highly crystalline with hexagonal packing in ab planes ( $\stackrel{\cdot}{a}=b=1.2$ nm).6c The peak at $2\Theta=30.2^{\circ}.$ , corresponding to the (001) reflection, suggests an ordered stacking with an interlayer spacing of ${\sim}2.88\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ . Thus, an AB slipped-parallel model is built according to the PXRD results,6c as shown in Fig. 1D. In Fig. 2A, the atomic force microscopy (AFM) image demonstrates a sheet structure and smooth surface (the arithmetic average roughness $\left(R_{a}\right)$ is about $0.23\mathsf{n m}_{.}^{\prime}$ for HHB-Cu NSs. The thickness of the NSs was determined as $4.2{\pm}1.1\mathsf{n m}$ and the lateral size was $0.30\mathrm{-}0.65~\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ (Fig. 2B, Fig. S1†). SEM images also show an inherent sheet structure of HHB-Cu NSs, which is consistent with the AFM observation (Fig. S2†).
EDX mapping and spectrum (Fig. S3 and ${\mathsf{S}}4^{\dagger}$ ) demonstrate a uniform distribution of C, Cu, O, and Na in the achieved HHB-Cu NSs. A Cu:O ratio of 1.2:4 was determined, which is consistent with X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization (1.1:4) and very close to the calculated value for $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ units (1:4) (Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{5}}^{\dagger}.$ ). The XPS $\mathtt{C u2p3/2}$ peak at $933\mathsf{e V}$ and the satellite peaks at 932 and 943 eV indicate that Cu exhibits two oxidation states of $+2$ and $+1$ with a $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{+}$ ratio of about 9.44. As shown in Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}6{\mathsf{A}}^{\dagger}$ , the Cu $K\cdot$ -edge of X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) in HHB-Cu NSs is completely different from those of $\mathtt{C u O}$ and Cu foil. The pre-edge feature in HHB-Cu NSs originates from the transition of 1s core electrons to hybridized orbitals of $3d$ . In addition, it can be seen that the white‐line intensity of HHB-Cu NSs is slightly lower than that of CuO, indicating that $\mathsf{c u}$ species is in two oxidation forms in HHB-Cu NSs, consistent with the XPS results. Accordingly, the framework of
HHB-Cu NSs possesses several redox states with up to two electron transfers per $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ unit (Scheme ${\mathsf{S}}2^{\dagger}$ ), which acts as active sites for a promising energy storage function. The measured extended X‐ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra show that there is one prominent peak at $\sim1.52\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ from the Cu-O contribution in HHB-Cu NSs (Fig. S6B†), while another sample CuO clearly exhibits two different predominant peaks at $\sim1.56\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and ${\sim}2.49\mathrm{\AA},$ which arise from $c\mathsf{u}{-}0$ and $\mathsf{C u-C u}$ bonds, respectively. These results provide strong proof on the formation of square planar complexes via the coordination of THQ and $\mathsf{c u}$ ions and confirm the absence of metal oxides such as CuO and Cu foil in the HHB-Cu NSs.
Raman spectra of HHB-Cu NSs manifest the elimination of OH and $c=0$ peaks at $3370\ c m^{-1}$ and $1649\ c m^{-1}$ , respectively, which confirm the efficient coordination between Cu and O (Fig. S7†). The UV/vis absorption spectra of the dispersed HHB-Cu NSs on a quartz substrate show a broad absorption band in the near-IR region, suggesting an absorption edge of ${\sim}1.7$ eV (Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{8}}^{\dagger}$ ; also see Tauc plots of $(a h v)^{2}$ vs hν). Based-on the low-pressure ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurements at $77~\mathsf{K},$ the BET surface area of HHB- $\mathtt{\cdot c u}$ NSs was estimated to be as high as $385\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. ${\sf S9^{\dagger}}.$ , due to the existence of more exposed surface between layers as a result of the high crystallinity, large domain size, and nanoscale thicknesses. Without using SDS (Fig. S10†), the contrast synthesis with the same conditions resulted in layer-stacked HHB-Cu powders with lower crystallinity, smaller domain size (Fig. S11†), in which the surface was deeply buried, leading to lower accessible surface area (BET surface area, $119\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1})$ (Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{9}}^{\dagger},$ ).
To gain insight into the crystallinity of the resultant HHB-Cu NSs, we performed selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) and aberration-corrected high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (AC-HRTEM) imaging. The SAED pattern reveals a hexagonal symmetry with sharp diffraction spots (Fig. 2C). The firstorder reflections were found at $0.93\ n m^{-1}.$ , corresponding to a hexagonal unit cell with a side length of $1.24\:\mathsf{n m}$ (inset in Fig. 2C), agreeing with the lattice parameters of HHB-Cu.6c The SAED patterns acquired on isolated HHB- $\mathtt{\cdot c u}$ NSs manifest single crystallographic orientation (Fig. S12†), revealing the single-crystalline nature of the HHB-Cu NSs. Furthermore, AC-HRTEM imaging unambiguously illustrates the highly ordered hexagonal MOF network with a nearatomic resolution of $0.22{\mathsf{n m}}$ . Strikingly, no point or line defects have been observed within the field-of-view, further evidencing the high crystallinity of the HHB-Cu NSs (Fig. 2D). The measured pore size on the magnified AC-HRTEM image was $1.2\ \mathsf{n m}$ , which is in perfect agreement with the structural model (Fig. 2E).
The versatility of the surfactant-assisted solution synthesis method using SDS was investigated through the preparation of a variety of 2D $c$ -MOF NSs including HHB-Ni NSs and HHTP-Cu NSs $(\mathsf{H H T P}{=}2,3,6,7,10,;$ 11-hexahydroxytriphenylene). Structural and morphological characterizations by PXRD, TEM, SAED, and AFM measurements revealed that the synthetic HHB-Ni presented the single-crystalline ultrathin NSs with lateral size of $0.25\substack{-0.56\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}}$ and thickness of $4.5{\pm}1.4~\mathsf{n m}$ ${\bf\tilde{\rho}}^{\prime}{\bf\tilde{\rho}}^{\prime}{\bf\tilde{\rho}}^{\prime}$ layers) (Fig. 1C, Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}13^{\dagger}$ ). In addition, the obtained HHTP-Cu NSs that synthesized with surfactant show a single-crystalline feature with a lateral size of $0.002\cdot0.02\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ and a thickness of $5.1{\pm}2.6\$ nm ${\sim}10$ layers) (Fig. 1E and 1F, Fig. ${\mathsf{S}}14^{\dagger}$ ), whereas bulk powder with a rod like morphology was achieved in the absence of surfactant.
