The electrochemical performance of HHB-Cu NSs was then evaluated in coin cells with Li metal as the counter electrode. The electrolyte is $1\ M\ \mathsf{L i P F}_{6}$ in ethylene carbonate (EC)/ethyl methyl carbonate (EMC) $\left(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}=1;1\right)$ . The cyclic voltammetry (CV) profiles of HHB-Cu NSs exhibit a couple of redox peaks within the potential window of 1.3 to 2.6 V (vs Li/Li+) (Fig. 3A, Fig. S19†), which suggest reversible transformation between the neutral and negatively charged (reduced) states of HHB-Cu NSs with the insertion/desertion of Li ions. In the galvanostatic charge-discharge test at $1.3\substack{-2.6\vee},$ the HHB-Cu NSs electrode delivers a specific capacity of $153\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $0.1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. $S20^{\dagger}.$ . The charge-discharge curves present a single charge/discharge plateau, matching very well with the CV result. After 50 cycles at $0.1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the reversible capacity could be stabilized at 122 mA h $\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. S21†). Under extended potential windows of 1.3- $3.3\mathsf{V}$ and $1.3–4.0\mathsf{V}.$ , the specific capacity of the HHB-Cu NSs electrode can be pushed up to 300 and $400\ \mathsf{m A h}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which is superior to those of the other reported MOFs $(66-155\mathsf{m A h g^{-1}}$ ) (Table
S2, Fig. $S22^{+}]$ . These capacities correspond the full utilization of $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ units $(\mathsf{C u O_{4}}^{0}/\mathsf{C u O_{4}}^{2-}$ ; Scheme ${\mathsf{S}}3^{\dagger}.$ ) and $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ $(\mathsf{C u O}_{4}^{0}/\mathsf{C u O}_{4}^{2-})/\mathsf{C u}$ center $(\mathsf{C u}^{2+}/\mathsf{C u}^{+})$ .22 However, after tens of cycles, the capacity decayed below 200 mA h ${\pmb g}^{-1}$ , corresponding to a low capacity retention of $36.0\substack{-66.7\%}$ . The above results suggest that HHB-Cu NSs electrode exhibits better electrochemical reversibility under a oneelectron redox process at $1.3\substack{-2.6\vee}$ $(\mathsf{C u O_{4}}^{-}/\mathsf{C u O_{4}}^{2}\cdot$ -; Scheme ${\mathsf{S}}2^{\dagger}$ ) than under a multi-electron process at 1.3-3.3 V and at $1.3–4.0\mathsf V.$ . It can be explained by the fact that less strain will be generated in the framework of HHB-Cu NSs under the one-electron process, thus leading to higher structural/cycling stability.
The rate capabilities of the HHB-Cu NSs electrode were further evaluated under various current densities. Clearly, enhancing the current rates from 0.1 to $2.0\textsf{A g}^{-1}$ slightly increases the chargedischarge polarization (Fig. 3B). Specific capacities of 122, 111, 105, 100, 99 and $94\mathrm{\mA}\mathsf{h g}^{-1}$ could still be retained at 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.8, 1.0 and $2.0\textsf{A g}^{1}$ , respectively (Fig. 3C). We attribute the high rate performance of HHB-Cu NSs to its ultrathin morphology, which provides short pathways for the fast ion/electron diffusion in the electrode. The long-term cycling stability of HHB-Cu NSs electrode was also surveyed at $1.0\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (charge in $6~\mathrm{{min}}$ ). After 1000 cycles, high capacity retention of $90\%$ was achieved with $100\%$ Coulombic efficiency (Fig. 3D), suggesting robust structure stability of HHB-Cu NSs under the electrochemical environment and high redox reversibility of active sites. The cycling stability of our HHB-Cu NSs is the record performance among the reported MOFs (Table S2, Supporting Information).
For comparison, the electrochemical behavior of bulk HHB-Cu particles were also evaluated under the same condition. Compared to HHB-Cu NSs, the bulk HHB-Cu represented a much smaller CV area and larger peak voltage gaps, indicating lower capacity/active site utilization and higher polarization (Fig. 4A). At 1.3-2.6 V (Scheme S2 †), the bulk HHB-Cu exhibited a limited capacity of $40{-}50\mathsf{m A h g^{-1}}$ (Fig. S20†), which could be ascribed to the sluggish ion diffusion and low utilization $(13.4\%)$ of active sites resulting from a long-rang parallelstacking structure (Fig. 4B). The rate capabilities of bulk HHB-Cu exhibits huge polarization once upon increasing the current densities (Fig. S23A†), therefore sacrificing most capacities. Low capacity of 4.6 mAh ${\pmb g}^{-1}$ of bulk HHB-Cu was retained at $2.0\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ , which was only 11.5 $\%$ of the value obtained at $0.1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. $S23\mathsf{B}^{\dagger}$ ).
To further understand the electrochemical performance, and to evaluate the merit of HHB-Cu NSs in ion/electron diffusion, we performed electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements on HHB-Cu NSs and bulk HHB-Cu. The obtained Nyquist plots are shown in Fig. 4C. Obviously, HHB-Cu NSs display a much lower charge transfer resistance $\left(R_{c t}\right)$ of ${\sim}85$ Ω when compared to bulk HHB-Cu $(R_{c t}=\sim216\Omega)$ , and an almost vertical low-frequency region represents a lower ionic resistance. These results confirm the substantial contribution of the ultrathin nature of HHB-Cu NSs in the fast charge/discharge process and the high utilization of active sites (Fig. 4D). Due to the open porosity and thin sheet morphology, the Li+ ion diffusion along the channels most probably dominates as the main path for $\mathsf{L i^{+}}$ insertion into HHB-Cu NSs rather than diffusion between layers. In support of the redox mechanism, we further compared the electrochemical properties of HHB-Cu NSs and HHB-Ni NSs. Clearly, the CV profile of HHB-Cu NSs is characterized by two distinct peaks at 1.64 and $\begin{array}{r}{1.93\mathrm{~V},}\end{array}$ whereas the HHB-Ni NSs have a substantially broadened peak region in the potential range from 1.9 to 2.5 V (Fig. S24†). As a result, we highlight that the active site should be mainly situated on the $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ unit, which dominates the electrochemical behavior of HHB-Cu MOFs.5 On the other hand, detailed studies were further implemented by the ex-situ Fourier transformed infrared (FT-IR) spectrum. As shown in Fig. S25†, the peak at ${\sim}1515~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , assigned to $c=c$ bond from benzene ring, disappeared at discharged state, suggesting the disappearance of the conjugation of ${\mathsf C}_{6}$ ring.23 In addition, a reversible change of a peak at ${\sim}1068\ c m^{-1}$ (attributed to C-O bond) during the charge and discharge processes was observed, confirming the Li-storage activity of $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ units. The proposed evolution of electronic states of the $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ unit during the charge/discharge process is shown in Fig. 4E.
## Conclusions
In summary, we demonstrated a universal strategy through a surfactant-assisted solution synthesis method to prepare ultrathin 2D $c$ -MOFs nanosheets with few-layered structures, large lateral sizes (aspect ratio exceeding 200), and singlecrystalline features in large scale and high yield. The synthetic HHB-Cu NSs as a showcase were utilized as cathode materials in Li-ion battery, which facilitated the utilization efficiency of redox sites and allowed faster ion/electron kinetics due to the improved surface area and significantly shortened ion/electron diffusion pathways compared to bulk HHB-Cu particles. Thus, HHB-Cu NSs presented greatly improved Li-ion storage performance with a specific capacity of 153 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and exhibited high rate capability (charge within $3\min$ , $81\%$ , 1.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ vs. $0.1\textsf{A g}^{-1})$ and long-term cycling stability $90\%$ capacity retention after 1000 cycles), which are superior to that of the state-of-the-art MOFs. Our work highlights an approach towards solution-processible ultrathin crystalline nanosheets of 2D $c$ -MOFs and paves the way for the development of other devices, such as electronics, sensors.
